## 5. CAN‑FD Communication Protocol

We use CAN‑FD with up to 64‑byte payload to send all control commands in a single frame, eliminating reassembly issues.

**Control Command (ID = 0x100, from Jetson, 500 Hz):**

| Byte(s) | Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0–3 | timestamp_us | uint32 | µs since STM32 sync epoch |
| 4 | thrust | uint8 | 0–255 → 0.0–1.0 normalized |
| 5–6 | torque_x | int16 | Torque x (scaled: int16 = N·m × 1000) |
| 7–8 | torque_y | int16 | Torque y |
| 9–10 | torque_z | int16 | Torque z |
| 11–63 | reserved | — | Future use (capsule gates, etc.) |

**Heartbeat (ID = 0x301, 1 kHz):** timestamp (4 B) + status (1 B: PC‑SRGI active, thermal throttle, low battery).  
**Telemetry:** Multiple IDs (0x200–0x20F) for position, velocity, attitude, angular velocity, capsule state, etc. Each includes a timestamp for synchronization.

**Time sync:** STM32 broadcasts a sync frame (ID 0x000) every 100 ms with its 64‑bit µs timestamp. The Jetson corrects its clock via linear regression.

