## 7. Neural Architecture: Physics‑Primitive Capsules

### 7.1 Physical Feature Extraction

From the current state and control, we compute a fixed set of 20 physically interpretable features \( \xi \in \mathbb{R}^{20} \) (detailed in Table 1). These are used as inputs to the capsule primitives, ensuring each capsule acts on a meaningful scalar quantity.

**Table 1: Physical feature vector \( \xi \)**

| Index | Feature | Formula | Scaling | Typical Range |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Body velocity x | \( v_{b,x} = (R^\top v)_x \) | 1/10 | ±1.5 |
| 2 | Body velocity y | \( v_{b,y} \) | 1/10 | ±1.5 |
| 3 | Body velocity z | \( v_{b,z} \) | 1/5 | ±2 |
| 4 | Speed | \( \|v_b\| \) | 1/15 | 0–1 |
| 5 | Angle of attack proxy | \( v_{b,z} / (\sqrt{v_{b,x}^2+v_{b,y}^2}+\epsilon) \) | — | ±1 |
| 6 | Angular rate x | \( \omega_x \) | 1/5 | ±1 |
| 7 | Angular rate y | \( \omega_y \) | 1/5 | ±1 |
| 8 | Angular rate z | \( \omega_z \) | 1/5 | ±1 |
| 9 | Angular rate norm | \( \|\omega\| \) | 1/8 | 0–1 |
|10 | Altitude AGL | \( z_{\text{AGL}} \) | 1/5 | 0–2 |
|11 | Vertical speed | \( \dot{z}_{\text{AGL}} \) (EKF) | 1/3 | ±2 |
|12 | Dynamic pressure | \( \frac{1}{2}\rho \|v_b\|^2 \) | 1/100 | 0–1.38 |
|13 | Thrust command | \( T_{\text{cmd}} \) (0‑1) | 10 | 0–10 |
|14 | Torque cmd x | \( \tau_x \) (N·m) | 100 | ±2 |
|15 | Torque cmd y | \( \tau_y \) (N·m) | 100 | ±2 |
|16 | Torque cmd z | \( \tau_z \) (N·m) | 100 | ±2 |
|17 | Torque norm | \( \|\tau\| \) (N·m) | 100 | 0–3.5 |
|18 | Body accel residual | \( a_{\text{res}} = \dot{v}_b - g \) (filtered) | 1/10 | ±1 |
|19 | Advance ratio proxy | \( \lambda = \frac{\|v_{b,xy}\|}{k_\omega \sqrt{T_{\text{cmd}} + \epsilon}} \) | 1/0.5 | 0–2 |
|20 | Constant | 1.0 | — | 1.0 |

*Feature 19:* Rotor angular velocity is estimated from the static thrust curve \( T_{\text{cmd}} T_{\text{max}} \approx 4 k_T \omega_{\text{prop}}^2 \). With \( T_{\text{max}} = 15 \) N, \( k_T \approx 1.2\times10^{-5} \) N/RPM², we obtain \( \omega_{\text{prop}} \approx 400\sqrt{T_{\text{cmd}}} \) rad/s. The constant \( k_\omega = 0.089 \cdot 400 \approx 35.6 \) m/(rad·s). The proxy is thus \( \lambda = \|v_{b,xy}\| / (35.6\sqrt{T_{\text{cmd}}+0.01}) \).  
*Feature 18:* The body acceleration residual (EKF estimated linear acceleration minus gravity) is low‑pass filtered at 20 Hz and provides a direct indicator of the total external force.

All features are scaled to keep the input to the primitives within ≈ [‑1, 1] for stable training.

### 7.2 Transformer Context Encoder

A lightweight transformer processes the last \( H=5 \) time steps of raw state and control. Each step input \( [s_t, u_t] \in \mathbb{R}^{17} \) is projected to \( d_{\text{model}}=64 \), added to learned positional encodings. The encoder has \( L=2 \) layers with 4 attention heads, feed‑forward size 256, GELU activation. The output is the **embedding of the last time step**, providing a causal summary \( h \in \mathbb{R}^{64} \).

### 7.3 Capsule Layer

We define 12 capsules, each associated with a specific physical primitive and a designated feature from Table 1.

**For capsule i:**
- A learnable scalar weight \( w_i \) and bias \( b_i \) are applied to the assigned feature \( \xi_{k_i} \).
- The primitive \( \phi_i \) (Table 2) produces a scalar intensity \( \psi_i = \phi_i(w_i \xi_{k_i} + b_i) \).
- The gate logit \( \gamma_i = \text{Linear}_i(h) \) (a linear projection from the context vector).
- Gate activation \( z_i = \sigma(\gamma_i / \tau) \), where temperature \( \tau \) is annealed from 1.0 to 0.1 during training.
- The capsule contributes to the 6‑D residual force/torque vector as \( \mathbf{F}_i = z_i \cdot (\mathbf{v}_i \cdot \psi_i) \), where \( \mathbf{v}_i \in \mathbb{R}^6 \) is a learnable basis vector.

Total neural residual: \( F_{\text{neural}} = \sum_{i=1}^{12} \mathbf{F}_i \).

**Table 2: Capsule primitives and assigned features**

| Cap | Primitive \( \phi \) | Formula | Feature \( \xi_k \) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Identity | \( x \) | 12 (Dynamic pressure) |
| 2 | Quadratic | \( x^2 \) | 12 (Dynamic pressure) |
| 3 | Sinusoidal | \( \sin(\omega x + \varphi) \) | 10 (Altitude AGL) |
| 4 | Gaussian | \( \exp(-x^2/\sigma^2) \) | 10 (Altitude AGL) |
| 5 | Rational | \( x/(1+|x|) \) | 4 (Speed) |
| 6 | Sigmoid | \( \sigma(x) \) | 5 (Angle of attack) |
| 7 | Identity | linear drag | 4 (Speed) |
| 8 | Quadratic | quadratic drag | 4 (Speed) |
| 9 | Sinusoidal | periodic disturbance | 9 (Angular rate norm) |
|10 | Gaussian | localized torque effect | 9 (Angular rate norm) |
|11 | Rational | saturation | 19 (Advance ratio) |
|12 | Identity | bias / residual constant | 20 (Constant) |

Capsules may share primitives but act on different features or with different learned parameters. The assignment is initialised to reflect known physics but can adapt during training.

### 7.4 Energy‑Complexity Penalty

During training, the total “computational cost” of the capsule layer is:
\[
E_{\text{comp}} = \sum_{i=1}^{12} \big( c_{\text{base}} + c_{\text{active}} z_i \big),
\]
with \( c_{\text{base}} = 0.01 \), \( c_{\text{active}} = 0.1 \). This encourages sparse gating.

