## Appendix H: Thermal and Power Management

### H.1 Passive Cooling Design
The Jetson Orin Nano is mounted on a custom copper heatsink (50×50×20 mm) with a thermal interface pad (5 W/m·K). The heatsink is positioned in the propeller downwash. CFD analysis on a similar 7″ frame shows a junction‑to‑ambient thermal resistance of ≈3.5 °C/W at hover. At 5 W TDP and 40 °C ambient, \( T_j = 40 + 5\times 3.5 = 57.5\,^\circ\text{C} \), well below the 80 °C throttle point. No fan is required.

### H.2 Dynamic Frequency and Capsule Scaling
A Rust monitor thread reads the Jetson’s on‑die temperature sensor every 500 ms. It computes a thermal headroom factor \( \alpha = \min(1, (80 - T_j)/20) \).  
- Control loop frequency is scaled to \( f = 500\,\alpha \) Hz (minimum 250 Hz).  
- At inference time, only the top \( \lceil \alpha \times 12 \rceil \) capsules with the largest gate values are evaluated; the rest are forced to zero.  

This ensures total compute load scales with the available thermal budget.

